-e: do not take the next word as the variable name with a POSIX getopt.

sshpass declares -e with an optional argument ("e::", the name of the
environment variable, SSHPASS by default). Optional arguments are a GNU
getopt extension; QNX's POSIX getopt treats "e::" as a required argument, so
"sshpass -e ssh host" read the variable name from "ssh" and failed ('-e
option given but "ssh" environment variable is not set'). When getopt hands
-e a separate word, give it back: GNU getopt never does that for an
optional argument, so the behaviour there is unchanged, and "-eNAME" still
names the variable everywhere.

Upstream-Status: QNX-specific

--- a/main.c
+++ b/main.c
@@ -166,6 +166,15 @@
             VIRGIN_PWTYPE;
 
             args.pwtype=PWT_PASS;
+            /* "e::" (an optional argument) is a GNU getopt extension. A POSIX
+             * getopt, QNX's, treats it as a required one and takes the next
+             * word ("-e ssh ...": the program name) as the variable name.
+             * GNU getopt never returns a separate word for an optional
+             * argument, so a separate word means it was not meant for -e. */
+            if( optarg!=NULL && optind>1 && optarg==argv[optind-1] ) {
+                optarg=NULL;
+                optind--;
+            }
             if( optarg==NULL )
                 optarg = "SSHPASS";
             args.orig_password=getenv(optarg);
